---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Zoom from '../../components/story/Zoom.astro';
import MatrixFlow from '../../components/diagrams/MatrixFlow.astro';
import '../../styles/story.css';
import graph from '../../assets/projects/matrix-graph.jpg';
import detail from '../../assets/projects/matrix-graph-detail.jpg';
import iphoneAsk from '../../assets/projects/matrix-iphone-ask.png';
import macAsk from '../../assets/projects/matrix-mac-ask.png';
import macTables from '../../assets/projects/matrix-mac-tables.png';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import { SITE } from '../../site';
const jsonld = { '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'SoftwareApplication', name: 'Matrix LLM', applicationCategory: 'BusinessApplication', operatingSystem: 'iOS, iPadOS, macOS', url: SITE.matrixApp, offers: { '@type': 'Offer', price: '0', priceCurrency: 'EUR' }, description: 'Ask your own documents on iPhone, iPad and Mac, offline. Every answer comes with the exact passage it is based on.', author: { '@type': 'Person', name: 'Matthias Nordwig' } };
---
<Base wide title="Matrix LLM: ask your documents, offline" description="Matrix LLM answers questions about your own PDFs, Word and Excel files and shows the passage each answer is based on. Free on the App Store for iPhone, iPad and Mac. The models run on the device." jsonld={jsonld} appStoreId={SITE.matrixAppId}>
<div class="story">

  <section class="beat" style="border-top:0">
    <div class="in hero-split">
      <div>
        <p class="kicker">Matrix LLM · iPhone, iPad and Mac</p>
        <h1>Ask your documents on your Mac or iPhone, offline.</h1>
        <p class="big">Add contracts, regulations or reports and ask in plain words. Every answer shows the exact
        passage it is based on. The models run on the device, so your documents stay there.</p>
        <a class="go" href={SITE.matrixApp}>Get it on the App Store</a>
        <p class="muted">Free to download for iPhone, iPad and Mac. The interface is in 22 languages.</p>
      </div>
      <figure class="phone">
        <Image src={iphoneAsk} alt="Matrix on an iPhone: a question about a sample contract, answered by Apple's on-device model, with the cited passage underneath." widths={[400, 800]} sizes="(min-width: 920px) 18rem, 80vw" loading="eager" />
        <figcaption>On the iPhone, Apple's model answers from a sample contract and quotes the passage.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="beat alt">
    <div class="in">
      <p class="kicker">The assistant</p>
      <h2>You can ask it to operate the app.</h2>
      <p class="big">You don't need to know what a vector or a knowledge graph is. Ask in plain words: "Create a collection
      called Contracts", "Check all supplier contracts for notice periods", "Switch to the Paper style". The assistant uses
      the same tools an AI agent gets and shows every step.</p>
      <div class="grid3">
        <div><h3>Asks before it acts</h3><p>Anything that changes something first appears as a card: what it will do and how much work it is, for example "About 40 model requests". It runs only after you tap Run.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Cannot delete</h3><p>Deleting is not one of its tools. The settings it may change come from a fixed list, never keys or endpoints.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Shows the result</h3><p>After a step it opens the result: the collection, the table run, the term in the graph, the document.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Built-in help</h3><p>"How do I add files?" is answered by any model from a built-in guide, with a "Show me" link to the right place.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Stops after two failures</h3><p>If the same action fails twice, it stops and says what went wrong and what you can do.</p></div>
        <div><h3>The limit</h3><p>Working the app needs a model that can call tools. Apple's on-device model answers the help questions, not the app tasks.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="beat">
    <div class="in">
      <p class="kicker">The first five minutes</p>
      <h2>From download to the first answer with sources.</h2>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li><div><h3>Pick a model</h3><p>Where the system offers it, Apple's model on the device needs no download. Otherwise one tap loads a small model. The search model downloads in the background; after that everything works offline.</p></div></li>
        <li><div><h3>Try a sample collection</h3><p>Three ready-made sets: a German sample contract, the official accident file of the US Airways flight that landed on the Hudson, and Wikipedia articles on the Panama Papers.</p></div></li>
        <li><div><h3>Add your own files</h3><p>PDF, Word, Excel, PowerPoint, e-mails, e-books, ZIP archives and photos (with text recognition). You can also share a file to Matrix from any other app.</p></div></li>
        <li><div><h3>Ask about a file right away</h3><p>Attach it to a question with the paper clip. Afterwards keep it as a collection or discard it.</p></div></li>
        <li><div><h3>Tap a citation</h3><p>It opens the exact passage; in PDFs, on its page.</p></div></li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="beat alt">
    <div class="in">
      <p class="kicker">The principle</p>
      <h2>The model reads. Everything that can be checked is plain code.</h2>
      <p class="big">The language model is used only where language has to be understood. Splitting
      documents, following references, verifying quotes, correcting sources and logging every step is
      done by ordinary code. It is reproducible, and no AI judgement is involved.</p>
      <p>On this page, <span class="who ai" style="margin:0">AI</span> means the model decides,
      <span class="who rules" style="margin:0">rules</span> means deterministic code does.</p>
      <div class="grid3">
        <div><h3>Every answer shows its sources</h3><p>Down to the verbatim quote in the original. You can click it and check it.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Your data stays local</h3><p>It runs with local models on the device or inside your own network. Nothing has to leave.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Everything is logged</h3><p>Every search, every hit and every automatic correction is logged and can be replayed.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="beat">
    <div class="in">
      <p class="kicker">Ask a question</p>
      <h2>It searches before it answers.</h2>
      <p class="big">You ask once, and the model searches the documents itself. Plain code sets its limits
      and checks the result.</p>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li><div><h3>Prepare the documents <span class="who rules">rules</span></h3><p>They are split along their real structure: articles, sections. Each piece gets an embedding, a vector that stands for its meaning, so search works on meaning and not only on words. References such as "pursuant to Art. 92(1)" are linked to their target.</p></div></li>
        <li><div><h3>Search, read, search again <span class="who ai">AI</span></h3><p>The model writes its own search queries, opens passages, follows references and searches again with the terms it found, until it has enough evidence or its budget is used up.</p></div></li>
        <li><div><h3>Guard rails <span class="who rules">rules</span></h3><p>A search budget caps the cost. Watchdogs stop it from going in circles. An answer given without any search is thrown away.</p></div></li>
        <li><div><h3>Answer only from what it found <span class="who ai">AI</span></h3><p>Every statement is tagged with its source number: [1], [2], [3].</p></div></li>
        <li><div><h3>Check the answer <span class="who rules">rules</span></h3><p>The checks in the next section run on every answer. Then everything is shown with clickable sources and a full log.</p></div></li>
      </ol>
      <div class="shot"><Zoom id="z-chat" src={macAsk} alt="Matrix on a Mac: a question about a sample contract, a two-sentence answer with quotes, and six source passages, the cited one marked." caption="On a Mac, Qwen3.6 35B runs on the device and answers from the sample contract: six sources, the cited one marked. The Prompt Inspector shows exactly what the model saw." /></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="beat alt">
    <div class="in split">
      <div>
        <p class="kicker">Citation checks</p>
        <h2>It checks every citation.</h2>
        <p class="big">After every answer, a series of plain-code checks goes over each citation.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <ol class="steps" style="margin-top:0">
          <li><div><h3>Does the source exist?</h3><p>Every [n] is checked against the list of sources that were actually retrieved.</p></div></li>
          <li><div><h3>Is the quote really there?</h3><p>Quoted words are searched for in the original. If they are not found, the answer gets a warning sign.</p></div></li>
          <li><div><h3>Does that source say it?</h3><p>Two independent signals: shared key terms (legal references count three times) and closeness in meaning.</p></div></li>
          <li><div><h3>Fix only when both agree</h3><p>Only then is the marker moved to the right source and labelled "→ 3". Otherwise there is just a hint. The answer text itself is never rewritten.</p></div></li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="beat">
    <div class="in">
      <p class="kicker">Tables</p>
      <h2>The same question for every row, as a table.</h2>
      <p class="big">Pick a collection or upload your own list and ask the same question for every row. Each answer has to
      fit the columns you define: text, number, date or choice.</p>
      <div class="shot"><Zoom id="z-tables" src={macTables} alt="Matrix Tables on a Mac: passages of a sample contract in rows, with answer columns Topic, Key point, Applies to and Date filled by the model." caption="Key facts of the sample contract, one row per passage. The tinted columns are the model's answers; the columns were suggested from the prompt." /></div>
      <div class="grid3">
        <div><h3>Answers are validated</h3><p>Missing or wrongly typed fields are shown in the cell.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Lists become rows</h3><p>Five items in one answer become five rows, each still linked to its source passage.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Redo single rows</h3><p>Run single rows again with a stronger model. Duplicates are merged. Export to Excel.</p></div>
      </div>
      <p class="muted" style="margin-top:2rem">Typical uses: a list of every obligation in a regulation with addressee, deadline and evidence ·
      the same check across hundreds of contracts · a first screening of a customer list.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="beat alt">
    <div class="in">
      <p class="kicker">The knowledge graph</p>
      <h2>Who relates to what, with a quote for every link.</h2>
      <p class="big">The model reads every passage and extracts things and their relations: who is responsible for what,
      what mitigates which risk, who owns whom. Every link carries a verbatim quote from the original.</p>
      <div class="shot"><Zoom id="z-detail" src={detail} alt="Close-up of the Matrix knowledge graph: entities such as risk analysis, human-rights training and water use, connected by relations like MITIGATES and APPLIES_TO." caption="Zoomed in: entities and typed relations such as MITIGATES, OWNS, APPLIES_TO." /></div>
      <div class="grid3">
        <div><h3>Quotes are verified</h3><p>A link whose quote can't be found is repaired first, not silently dropped. If the quote says the opposite ("must not…"), the link is flagged.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Duplicates merged</h3><p>"BaFin" and "Federal Financial Supervisory Authority" become one node. Every merge is logged and can be undone by hand.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Your own vocabulary</h3><p>The raw labels stay as they are. A switchable "lens" maps them onto your own vocabulary, without extracting again.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Topics and summaries</h3><p>The graph is split into clusters, each with a short summary, which works as a table of contents for the whole collection. Export to PowerPoint.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Failures are retried</h3><p>Passages that fail go to quarantine and are retried. Every finding goes to a review queue.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Quality depends on the model</h3><p>A local 30B-class model gives a fast first map, good for orientation but not proof. A frontier model gets close to "audit-ready".</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="beat">
    <div class="in split">
      <div>
        <p class="kicker">Platforms</p>
        <h2>One core for Mac, iPhone and browser.</h2>
        <p class="big">Everything important is in a single core written in Rust. The apps on top are thin.</p>
        <div class="grid3" style="grid-template-columns:1fr">
          <div><h3>iPhone and iPad</h3><p>Fully offline: search runs on the Neural Engine, chat runs with a local model on the GPU. Files come in through the share menu of any app.</p></div>
          <div><h3>Mac</h3><p>The same app. In addition, Claude Code and other AI agents can use the running app through MCP (Model Context Protocol, the standard way for agents to call tools).</p></div>
          <div><h3>Browser</h3><p>A Linux container in your own network. Users install nothing.</p></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="card"><MatrixFlow /></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="beat alt">
    <div class="in">
      <p class="kicker">Any model</p>
      <h2>Use any model, or only local ones.</h2>
      <p class="big">All model connections are managed in one place, and each is assigned to the task it does well.</p>
      <div class="grid3">
        <div><h3>On the device</h3><p>Apple's built-in model without a download, or models from Hugging Face, searched and loaded inside the app.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Search models</h3><p>Embedding and re-ranking models for search, also offline.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Company gateway</h3><p>OpenAI-compatible or Azure endpoints behind your own infrastructure.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Cloud providers</h3><p>Direct providers where that is allowed, chosen deliberately, case by case.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Limits per model</h3><p>Requests per minute, token budgets, parallel requests, so gateways and budgets are not overloaded.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Only what the model supports</h3><p>Tool use and reasoning are switched on only for models that support them.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="beat">
    <div class="in">
      <p class="kicker">When things fail</p>
      <h2>Tested against real outages.</h2>
      <div class="grid3">
        <div><h3>Load balancing</h3><p>Several endpoints work as one pool. Throttled ones are routed around before a fallback model takes over.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Streaming on slow gateways</h3><p>When a proxy gets slow, the app switches to streaming so the connection doesn't time out.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Retries</h3><p>Temporary errors are retried with growing waits. Cases that keep failing are skipped instead of stopping the run.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Fallback model</h3><p>If a model returns unusable output, a backup model takes over, only for that one passage.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Background jobs</h3><p>Long runs continue in the background with visible progress, and the app stays usable.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Temperature</h3><p>Local models watch the device's temperature and slow down instead of overheating a laptop.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="beat alt">
    <div class="in">
      <p class="kicker">By the numbers</p>
      <h2>What I measured.</h2>
      <div class="numbers">
        <div><strong>25 of 30</strong><span>test questions with the right passage among the first five hits, on 1,592 pages in four languages (14 as the very first hit)</span></div>
        <div><strong>56 ms</strong><span>for one search over 5,950 passages and 408 chart pages on a Mac</span></div>
        <div><strong>4,385</strong><span>entities extracted from a single annual report, each relation backed by a quote</span></div>
        <div><strong>22</strong><span>interface languages; questions and documents can be in different languages</span></div>
      </div>
      <p class="muted" style="margin-top:2rem">I track search quality with fixed sets of test questions and the passages each one should find. Quality is judged on those, not by impression.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="beat">
    <div class="in split">
      <div><p class="kicker">Also in Claude Code</p><h2>The same engine as a Claude Code plugin.</h2></div>
      <div>
        <p class="big">Claude Code can use Matrix as a tool. On 442 pages of EU regulations it answered in 42 instead of
        126 seconds per question on average, with less than half the input tokens.</p>
        <a class="go ghost" href="/projects/matrix-claude-code/">About the plugin</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="beat alt">
    <div class="in read">
      <p class="kicker">Where it stands</p>
      <h2>Free on the App Store.</h2>
      <p class="big">For iPhone, iPad and Mac. Asking and tables work with the model on the device. For the knowledge graph,
      a local model gives a quick first map and a stronger model a more reliable one. The checks run either way and show
      where the output can be trusted.</p>
      <a class="go" href={SITE.matrixApp}>Get it on the App Store</a>
      <a class="go ghost" href="mailto:matthias.nordwig@programmiere.de?subject=Matrix">Write to me</a>
      <p class="muted" style="margin-top:1.5rem">What the app stores and when anything leaves your device: <a href="/projects/matrix/privacy/">privacy policy</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

</div>
</Base>

<style>
  .hero-split { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 2.5rem; align-items: center; }
  @media (min-width: 920px) { .hero-split { grid-template-columns: 1.4fr 1fr; gap: 4rem; } }
  .hero-split .go { margin-right: .5rem; }
  .hero-split .muted { margin-top: 1rem; }
  .phone { margin: 0; max-width: 18rem; justify-self: center; }
  .phone :global(img) { width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; border-radius: 22px; box-shadow: 0 30px 60px -30px rgba(18, 20, 40, .45); }
  .phone figcaption { margin-top: .8rem; color: var(--muted); font-size: .9rem; }
</style>
